00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:03.750 align:start position:0%
in a historic move the city council in  asheville north carolina voted  unanimously  to approve a reparations resolution for  black residents  the resolution calls for the city to  make investment in areas where black  residents face disparities  it also asked the city manager to create  a plan to build  generational wealth and boost economic  mobility in  the black community the resolution also  includes a formal apology for the role  the city played in slavery  and enforcing racist policies and gail  we should note the resolution stopped  short of stipulating direct payments but  we're seeing momentum  for measures like this in places like  providence rhode island and california  and gail the fact that this is happening  in a former confederate state was noted  by keith young  one of the two african ameri american  member council members he said  hundreds of years of black blood spilled  that basically fills the cup we drink  from today  man i say go asheville north carolina  you know a lot of people are opening  themselves up to this conversation for  the very first time  i think it's important when you look at  the history you you'll have a better  understanding  about why so many people are pushing for  that so i think this is a good thing
 

